        </div>
        <div class="guide-box" style="border-left-color: #ffb703; background: #221e11;">
            <h3 style="color: #ffb703;">⚡ Electric Hob Masterclass</h3>
            <p><strong>1. Flat-Bottomed Pan:</strong> Use a flat-bottomed carbon steel wok or heavy cast-iron/stainless-steel skillet. Round woks wobble and lose up to 70% heat contact on electric rings.</p>
            <p><strong>2. 5-Minute Preheat:</strong> Electric elements take time to reach peak temperature. Turn your cooking zone to maximum high 4 to 5 minutes before cooking.</p>
            <p><strong>3. Single Batch Rule:</strong> Cook meat or wet vegetables in small single-portion batches (150g–200g). Overloading the pan causes a thermal drop, stewing food instead of frying it.</p>
            <p><strong>4. Two-Ring Control:</strong> Set one ring to Max for searing/stir-frying, and a neighboring ring to Medium/Low for simmering curries and thickening sauces.</p>
            <p><strong>5. Wok Hei Splash:</strong> Splash 1 tbsp of Shaoxing wine down the screaming-hot inner wall of the pan right before taking it off the heat for authentic takeaway aroma.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

